---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (p = '') => (base.endsWith('/') ? base : base + '/') + p;
---

<Guide
  title="What the Bhagavad Gita says about anger"
  description="The Bhagavad Gita traces exactly how anger takes hold — from desire to fury to clouded judgment — and how to loosen its grip. The key verses, explained."
  slug="bhagavad-gita-on-anger"
  datePublished="2026-09-17"
  dateModified="2026-10-17"
  faq={[
    {
      q: 'What does the Bhagavad Gita say about anger?',
      a: 'The Gita treats anger as a predictable chain reaction, not a moral failing: dwelling on something breeds attachment, attachment becomes desire, and thwarted desire becomes anger, which then clouds judgment and memory (verses 2.62–2.63). Understanding the chain lets you catch it early.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which Bhagavad Gita verse is about anger?',
      a: 'Verses 2.62–2.63 map how anger arises and what it destroys. Verse 2.56 praises the sage free from anger, and 16.21 calls anger one of the three "gates" — alongside desire and greed — that lead a person to ruin.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How does the Bhagavad Gita say to control anger?',
      a: 'By interrupting the chain early — noticing the fixation before it hardens into craving and fury — and by steadying the mind through practice and non-attachment (2.56, 6.35). The Gita aims not at suppressing anger but at not being swept away by it.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does the Gita call anger a gate to ruin?',
      a: 'In verse 16.21, desire, anger and greed are named the three gates to self-destruction because each hands your judgment over to a craving. Anger in particular, 2.63 says, leads to delusion, loss of memory and the collapse of clear reason.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Anger gets unusually clear treatment in the Bhagavad Gita. Krishna doesn't moralize about it — he
    diagnoses it, tracing the exact steps by which a calm mind ends up in flames, and pointing to
    where the chain can be broken.
  </p>

  <h2>Where anger comes from</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's most quoted passage on anger describes it as the child of thwarted desire — not a
    first cause but a link in a chain.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Dwelling on the objects of the senses breeds attachment; from attachment springs desire, and from desire comes anger."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/62')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.62</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Anger, in other words, is what desire does when it's blocked. This is oddly freeing: if you can
    catch the fixation earlier — while it's still just wanting — you never reach the fire.
  </p>

  <h2>Why unchecked anger is so costly</h2>
  <p>
    The next verse follows the chain to its end, and it reads like a description of every decision
    regretted in the heat of the moment.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"From anger comes confusion; from confusion, loss of memory; from loss of memory, the ruin of judgment — and then one is lost."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/63')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.63</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Anger doesn't just feel bad — it takes your clarity with it. That's why the Gita treats loosening
    its grip as a matter of protecting your own good judgment, not just being nice.
  </p>

  <h2>Anger as a gate to ruin</h2>
  <p>
    Later, the Gita names anger among three forces to turn away from decisively.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Lust, anger and greed — these are the three gates to self-ruin. Therefore let them go."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/16/21')}>Bhagavad Gita 16.21</a></cite>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What steadiness looks like instead</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's alternative isn't suppression — it's a mind no longer at the mercy of what it wants
    and dreads. The <a href={withBase('gita/2/56')}>sage of steady wisdom (2.56)</a> is "free from
    attachment, fear and anger," and <a href={withBase('gita/5/23')}>Chapter 5</a> praises the one
    who can withstand the push of desire and anger before letting go of the body. Freedom from anger
    is less about winning a fight with it and more about not lighting the fuse.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to read next</h2>
  <p>
    The heart of this teaching is in <a href={withBase('gita/2')}>Chapter 2</a>. For the wider map of
    the qualities that free or bind us, see <a href={withBase('gita/16')}>Chapter 16</a>. And when
    anger is actually present, opening a verse for that moment can be steadier than reading straight
    through.
  </p>
</Guide>
